            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Weekly Observation: Structural Shift in Creative Traffic</h2>
<p>This week saw notable cost fluctuations for cold launches across TikTok Shop categories. One home goods team reported a 15%-20% drop in first-week conversion rates compared to last month, even after adjusting for seasonal factors. The core issue points to creative homogenization and shallow creative pools.</p>
<p>The platform algorithm now favors high-engagement content, meaning product showcase videos receive less organic traction. Cold launches can no longer rely on "one video to rule them all"—a tiered creative asset pool is now essential from day one.</p>
<h2>Three Common Creative Pain Points in Cold Launches</h2>
<p>Based on feedback from teams this week, cold launches typically struggle with three creative management issues:</p>
<p><strong>1. Insufficient Test Creative Volume</strong><br />
Some teams launch with only 3-5 creatives, leaving no room for error. The platform recommends 15-20 initial test creatives for new product launches.</p>
<p><strong>2. No Creative Tiering</strong><br />
All creatives receive equal budget, lacking separation between "traffic-driving creatives," "conversion creatives," and "branding creatives." This drags down top performers while wasting budget on underperformers.</p>
<p><strong>3. Missing Creative Repurposing</strong><br />
When a creative outperforms the benchmark by 30%, teams fail to quickly generate variations, causing high-performing assets to burn out prematurely.</p>
<h2>This Week's Practical Steps for Building Your Creative Pool</h2>
<p>Targeting the issues above, here's a proven workflow for new product cold launches:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Step 1: Account Speed Testing to Define Creative Direction</strong><br />
Select 3 validated high-engagement account styles and post 2 test creatives each over 3 days. Monitor completion rates and click-through rates. This week's data shows creatives with completion rates above 40% enter the priority pool.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2: Tier Your Creatives with Differentiated Budget Allocation</strong><br />
- <strong>Test Tier</strong>: $20-$30 per creative, focused on rapid filtering; promote winners upward.<br />
- <strong>主力层 (Main Tier)</strong>: Select 5-8 creatives with increased budgets of $50-$100 each.<br />
- <strong>爆款层 (Hit Tier)</strong>: Top 2-3 performers get 3-5 variation edits to maximize reach.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3: Establish a Creative Repurposing SOP</strong><br />
When a creative exceeds target ROAS by 1.5x, trigger the repurposing workflow:<br />
1. Keep the original running;<br />
2. Extract highlight clips for short-form content;<br />
3. Swap background music or subtitle style;<br />
4. Create different duration versions (15s/30s/60s) for various ad placements.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Three Recommendations from This Week's Editorial Team</h2>
<ol>
<li>
<p><strong>Prepare Creatives in Advance</strong>: Don't start shooting when the product launches. Complete initial edits 2 weeks prior to launch, enabling immediate testing. One utility app team cut their cold launch cycle by 5 days using this approach.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Monitor Creative Lifecycles</strong>: Individual creatives typically perform well for 2-3 weeks. Prepare replacements before the lifecycle ends to avoid gaps.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Leverage Platform Creative Tools</strong>: TikTok Creative Center updates trending audio and hashtags weekly. This week's #TikTokShopDeals topic shows conversion rates above average—making it a viable reference direction for new product launches.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<p><strong>Next Week's Preview</strong>: We'll dive into TikTok Shop's ad product updates this month and discuss their potential impact on user acquisition strategies.</p>
